[38;5;231m⏺[39m Got it. The filters need to cover three jobs: finding failed sign-ins, finding changes to security settings, and
  looking at a single week. So the plan is an event type filter with two shortcuts plus a from/to date range.

  I've drawn three placement options in a browser tab so they're easier to compare:
  [94m]8;id=k3r8r0;http://localhost:51920/?key=4c2acb6ee9106d76101ffbe4557e6a17b6b16cd8206d9fe4dab4fac7afbb2159\http://localhost:51920/?key=4c2acb6ee9106d76101ffbe4557e6a17b6b16cd8206d9fe4dab4fac7afbb2159[39m]8;;\ (The browser tab uses a
  lot of tokens, so tell me if you'd rather keep this in the terminal.)

  - [1mA. Filter bar above the table:[0m every control is always visible, including all nine type checkboxes, and it takes
    about three rows of height.
  - [1mB. Compact row:[0m the shortcuts and the from/to dates sit in one row, the nine type checkboxes are behind a "More
    filters" popover, and active filters show as removable tags.
  - [1mC. Sidebar:[0m the filters stay in view while you scroll, but the table gets cramped at the page's 960px width.

  I'd go with B. Your three jobs each take one click or one date pick, and the full type list stays out of the way until
  someone needs it. A is a close second and a bit less code, because it has no popover.

  Have a look and tell me what you think here. You can click an option in the browser to select it.

